(a)
(i) Editing: The process of selecting, arranging, cutting, and assembling recorded footage (video and audio) into a coherent, finished film or programme. It involves making decisions about the sequence, pacing, and rhythm of shots to tell a story effectively. [2 marks]
(ii) Scene transitions: Techniques used to move from one scene or shot to the next in a film, controlling the flow and rhythm of the narrative. Common transitions include cuts, fades, dissolves, wipes, and cross-cuts. [2 marks]

(b) Example software: Adobe Premiere Pro / DaVinci Resolve / Final Cut Pro. Any four tools:
(1) Timeline — a visual workspace where video and audio clips are arranged in sequence to build the edit; editor can trim, rearrange, and layer clips;
(2) Razor/Blade tool — used to cut clips at specific points on the timeline, allowing the editor to remove unwanted sections;
(3) Transition effects panel — provides pre-built transition options (dissolves, wipes, fades) that can be dragged between clips on the timeline;
(4) Colour grading/correction tools (e.g. Lumetri Colour in Premiere, Colour Wheels in DaVinci) — used to adjust exposure, contrast, saturation, and apply colour grades to establish mood;
(5) Audio mixer — controls volume levels, panning, and audio effects for each audio track in the timeline;
(6) Export/render panel — used to output the finished edit in a chosen format, resolution, and codec for delivery;
(7) Effects library — a collection of visual effects (blur, glow, titles, graphics) that can be applied to clips. [2 marks per well-described tool, up to 4 tools]

(c) Offline editing importance: (1) Allows editors to work with low-resolution proxy files, reducing strain on computer hardware during the creative editing process; (2) Editors can make creative decisions about structure, pacing, and story without needing the final high-quality files; (3) More cost-effective as it does not require expensive high-end post-production facilities; (4) Provides flexibility for experimentation and revision before committing to the final cut; (5) The edit can be done anywhere without needing access to the final digital files.

Online editing importance: (1) Involves working with the original high-resolution files to produce the final, broadcast/distribution-ready product; (2) Final colour grading, audio mixing, visual effects, and titles are applied at this stage for the highest quality output; (3) Ensures the final product meets technical delivery specifications required by broadcasters, festivals, or distributors; (4) Conforms the offline edit decision list (EDL) to the high-quality media for a polished finish. [Award 1 mark per valid point, balanced between both, up to 8]

